WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.181
A command is a saved prompt behind a slash.

00:00:03.531 --> 00:00:06.454
Type a slash and its name, and the agent runs it.

00:00:06.804 --> 00:00:12.715
Built-in ones run the session itself: /clear starts fresh, /compact summarizes,

00:00:12.715 --> 00:00:16.980
/context shows what's loaded, and /model switches models.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.599
Your own commands are now part of skills.

00:00:20.949 --> 00:00:27.850
A file at .claude/commands/deploy.md and a skill at .claude/skills/deploy

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.930
both give you /deploy.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:34.267
Whatever you type after the name arrives as $ARGUMENTS,

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:37.384
so /deploy staging passes the word staging.

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:42.641
Put one in your home .claude folder instead, and it follows you into every project.

00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:45.555
Here's that deploy command.

00:00:45.905 --> 00:00:49.120
Its frontmatter sets disable-model-invocation to true,

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.727
so only you can trigger it.

00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:53.769
Its body is just the steps, in plain words:

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:57.775
build, run the checks, then deploy to the environment you named.

00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:02.115
You don't want an agent deciding to deploy because the code looks ready.

00:01:02.465 --> 00:01:06.454
And until you run it, its description stays out of context entirely.

00:01:07.854 --> 00:01:11.587
The signal to make one: you've typed the same opening prompt again.

00:01:11.937 --> 00:01:16.716
Commit it to the project's .claude folder, and the whole team gets the same command.

00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:20.906
Keep anything with side effects, like deploys and commits, manual.

00:01:21.256 --> 00:01:25.864
Codex has its own built-ins, like /init, /review and /model.

00:01:26.214 --> 00:01:28.198
Its custom prompts are deprecated,

00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:31.057
so for your own commands in Codex, write a skill.
